# <%= it.ProjectName %>

This solution was generated by **create-clean-arch** — Next steps after project creation.

## Project structure

- `<%= it.ProjectName %>.Domain` — entities, enums, no dependencies
- `<%= it.ProjectName %>.Application` — CQRS commands/queries (MediatR), validation (FluentValidation), interfaces
- `<%= it.ProjectName %>.Infrastructure` — EF Core, repositories, external services
- `<%= it.ProjectName %>.Api` — ASP.NET Core Web API (controllers, middleware, DI wiring)
- `<%= it.ProjectName %>.Tests` — unit tests (xUnit + Moq)
<% if (it.IncludeWeb === "true") { %>- `<%= it.ProjectName %>.Web` — frontend (not part of the .sln; managed separately via npm)
<% } %>
## Getting started

```bash
dotnet restore
dotnet ef database update --project ./<%= it.ProjectName %>.Infrastructure --startup-project ./<%= it.ProjectName %>.Api
dotnet run --project ./<%= it.ProjectName %>.Api
```
<% if (it.IncludeWeb === "true") { %>
```bash
cd <%= it.ProjectName %>.Web
npm ci
npm run dev
```
<% } %>
## Configuration

Secrets are read from environment variables at startup rather than committed to `appsettings.json`:

- `APP_DB_CONNECTION` — appended to the SQL Server connection string
<% if (it.IncludeAuth === "true") { %>- `APP_JWT_SECRET` — JWT signing secret
- `APP_SMTP_USERNAME` / `APP_SMTP_PASSWORD` — SMTP credentials for password-reset email
<% } %>
Set these via your shell, a `.env` file (not committed), or your deployment platform's secret store — never commit real values into `appsettings*.json`.

## Adding a new feature

Once inside this project directory, generate a new vertical-slice feature (Commands/Queries/Handlers/Validators) with:

```bash
create-clean-arch generate feature <FeatureName>
```

See `Command.md` for the full EF Core migration and run command cheat-sheet.
